WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.403
-Our next guest is the
Emmy-winning writer and creator

00:00:03.403 --> 00:00:04.437
of "The Sopranos."

00:00:04.437 --> 00:00:05.872
His prequel film
to the iconic series,

00:00:05.872 --> 00:00:08.441
"The Many Saints of Newark,"
is in theaters and on HBO Max.

00:00:08.441 --> 00:00:09.508
Let's take a look.

00:00:09.508 --> 00:00:11.210
-Where is he?
-Downtown.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:13.713
-He's downtown, where he might
get burned to death?

00:00:13.713 --> 00:00:15.615
What are you people thinking?
-[ Crying ]

00:00:15.615 --> 00:00:17.483
-Janice, shut up with that!

00:00:17.483 --> 00:00:20.386
W-What were the two of you
doing at that card game?

00:00:20.386 --> 00:00:22.855
-Dad took Janice to ride the
rides while he played cards.

00:00:22.855 --> 00:00:24.791
But he wouldn't take me
because I pass gas,

00:00:24.791 --> 00:00:26.692
so he was eating lunch.

00:00:26.692 --> 00:00:29.962
-Get inside, both of you!

00:00:29.962 --> 00:00:32.198
-Please welcome to the show
David Chase.

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:34.033
Welcome, David.
How are you?

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:35.835
-I'm okay. How are you?
-I'm doing wonderful.

00:00:35.835 --> 00:00:37.937
I love this movie.

00:00:37.937 --> 00:00:40.306
I, of course,
was a huge fan of the show.

00:00:40.306 --> 00:00:42.641
When you were making it,
did you think to yourself,

00:00:42.641 --> 00:00:44.844
"This is a reward for
all my fans"

00:00:44.844 --> 00:00:47.213
or "this is an entry point
for new fans"

00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:49.716
or did you just have
a story to tell?

00:00:49.716 --> 00:00:55.221
-Uh...none of those.
-Okay.

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:57.056
-Warner Bros. wanted to do it.

00:00:57.056 --> 00:01:00.093
And they had been
coming to me over the years.

00:01:00.093 --> 00:01:03.862
And, finally, like 2018,
it sort of worked out,

00:01:03.862 --> 00:01:07.533
the timing was right,
and we came up with the story.

00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:09.902
-A lot's been written about how

00:01:09.902 --> 00:01:13.039
younger generations
are finding this show now.

00:01:13.039 --> 00:01:15.307
I -- When I first
watched it when it aired,

00:01:15.307 --> 00:01:17.844
I thought of it very much as
a show about the Mafia.

00:01:17.844 --> 00:01:21.714
And then I rewatched it with
my wife, who had never seen it,

00:01:21.714 --> 00:01:24.249
and I felt it was a show about

00:01:24.249 --> 00:01:26.585
a marriage
the second time through.

00:01:26.585 --> 00:01:29.088
What are you
hearing that younger people

00:01:29.088 --> 00:01:31.624
are being drawn to
about the show?

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:32.658
-I don't know.

00:01:32.658 --> 00:01:36.829
I hear a lot of
different things.

00:01:36.829 --> 00:01:42.769
And then, yesterday,
I finally had a lightning bolt

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:49.441
hit me that it's not PC,
and they don't get that anymore.

00:01:49.441 --> 00:01:53.479
And it seems like, "Wow!
Did you hear what he just say?"

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:55.381
I think that might be it.

00:01:55.381 --> 00:02:00.653
And the characters
are also un-PC.

00:02:00.653 --> 00:02:04.557
-Another thing that I feel like,
if you never watched the show --

00:02:04.557 --> 00:02:05.792
And I think part of this --

00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:07.326
I don't know what
you think of this theory.

00:02:07.326 --> 00:02:08.862
Because of the way
the show ended

00:02:08.862 --> 00:02:10.796
and how much, then,
people put into

00:02:10.796 --> 00:02:14.667
trying to take apart and
understand how the show ended,

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:17.737
people forgot that
it was a deeply funny show.

00:02:17.737 --> 00:02:19.706
And I'm wondering if
first-time viewers

00:02:19.706 --> 00:02:22.809
are so surprised by that,
because even I'll admit,

00:02:22.809 --> 00:02:24.877
I kind of forgot,
until I rewatched it,

00:02:24.877 --> 00:02:27.113
how there was never an episode

00:02:27.113 --> 00:02:30.116
that wasn't full of
comic moments.

00:02:30.116 --> 00:02:34.887
-Well, all I can say is
that I didn't real--

00:02:34.887 --> 00:02:38.157
I didn't -- I made a statement
to the effect that

00:02:38.157 --> 00:02:41.661
I don't know whether
it's a drama or a comedy.

00:02:41.661 --> 00:02:44.062
But that didn't really
hit me until, like,

00:02:44.062 --> 00:02:46.065
the show was almost over.

00:02:46.065 --> 00:02:47.700
That's when I realized.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.368
-Were you guys --
When you were writing it,

00:02:49.368 --> 00:02:51.237
were you laughing a lot
as you were working on the show?

00:02:51.237 --> 00:02:55.140
-Oh, yeah. Oh, yeah.
Yes, we were. We were.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:03:00.179
I mean, those characters
were just great, you know,

00:03:00.179 --> 00:03:01.481
pieces of clay to work with.

00:03:01.481 --> 00:03:04.550
They were -- You know,
Tony Sirico's funny,

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:06.252
just period.

00:03:09.488 --> 00:03:11.123
Everybody was, really.

00:03:11.123 --> 00:03:14.259
A.J. was funny. I don't know.
It was just -- it was just fun.

00:03:14.259 --> 00:03:17.697
It might have been the casting,
really, that did it.

00:03:17.697 --> 00:03:21.100
-Well, not only do I --
-Junior was a riot, you know?

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:22.601
-And the casting
not only was great,

00:03:22.601 --> 00:03:24.203
but I would imagine,
the longer you wrote it,

00:03:24.203 --> 00:03:27.506
the more fun people would have
writing the characters,

00:03:27.506 --> 00:03:30.075
Because you -- I mean, you must
have just gotten to know

00:03:30.075 --> 00:03:32.245
them as the show went on.

00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:37.684
-Yeah. I heard that from
some young woman

00:03:37.684 --> 00:03:39.219
at one of the events we went to.

00:03:39.219 --> 00:03:42.155
I said, "What do you guys
see in the show?"

00:03:42.155 --> 00:03:43.356
And she said, "I don't know.

00:03:43.356 --> 00:03:46.659
You come home from work
into your apartment,

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:48.595
and it's all dark
and you're exhausted.

00:03:48.595 --> 00:03:50.429
You turn it on,
and there they are.

00:03:50.429 --> 00:03:52.397
And you feel relaxed

00:03:52.397 --> 00:03:55.401
and like you're home,
like you're with your family."

00:03:55.401 --> 00:03:56.635
As far as I know,

00:03:56.635 --> 00:03:59.605
that's what television
has always been, right?

00:03:59.605 --> 00:04:02.341
In the '50s and '60s,
it was that same thing.

00:04:02.341 --> 00:04:04.210
Oh, here is that guy
that I know,

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:08.747
and he's going to do
his stupid line again.

00:04:08.747 --> 00:04:12.618
-The other thing I realized
watching it again is,

00:04:12.618 --> 00:04:15.154
when I watch "Mad Men,"
I want to have a drink,

00:04:15.154 --> 00:04:18.724
and when I watch "The Sopranos,"
I want a sandwich.

00:04:18.724 --> 00:04:20.993
-Yeah. Right. Right.

00:04:20.993 --> 00:04:23.563
-But, like, a sandwich that's,
like, wrapped in paper.

00:04:23.563 --> 00:04:25.498
Like, you almost never even
see the sandwich.

00:04:25.498 --> 00:04:27.666
I just like that part of it.

00:04:27.666 --> 00:04:30.136
-I understand. I get it.

00:04:32.972 --> 00:04:34.706
I get it.

00:04:34.706 --> 00:04:35.775
-I also want to ask --

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:37.910
Obviously, you know
the show had --

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:40.146
You know, music was
a huge part of the show.

00:04:40.146 --> 00:04:42.882
You know, Tony obviously
had music tastes that were very,

00:04:42.882 --> 00:04:44.250
you know,
specific to his character.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:47.654
I imagine they overlap with your
music tastes a great deal.

00:04:47.654 --> 00:04:49.722
But, for me,
there are certain songs

00:04:49.722 --> 00:04:52.458
that when I hear now,
my memory of them

00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:54.126
is tied to moments in
"The Sopranos."

00:04:54.126 --> 00:04:56.862
Like, when I hear "Glad Tidings"
by Van Morrison,

00:04:56.862 --> 00:04:58.597
I think Tony Blundetto's
about to get it.

00:04:58.597 --> 00:05:00.098
When I hear "Comfortably Numb,"

00:05:00.098 --> 00:05:01.968
I know that's sort of
the end of Christopher.

00:05:01.968 --> 00:05:03.969
And I'm wondering --
you put those songs there --

00:05:03.969 --> 00:05:08.174
can you hear those songs without
thinking of those scenes?

00:05:08.174 --> 00:05:09.942
-No, I can't.

00:05:09.942 --> 00:05:11.644
I mean, I said something about

00:05:11.644 --> 00:05:13.512
"Glad Tidings" the other day.

00:05:13.512 --> 00:05:15.281
If you said to me
"Glad Tidings,"

00:05:15.281 --> 00:05:19.252
I wouldn't necessarily
think about

00:05:19.252 --> 00:05:21.053
Tony Blundetto getting shot.

00:05:21.053 --> 00:05:23.356
If I heard it, I would.

00:05:23.356 --> 00:05:24.890
I would,
because I was very proud.

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:28.094
I never thought we would get
Van Morrison to do anything,

00:05:28.094 --> 00:05:32.164
and so I was really proud
and still am about that.

00:05:32.164 --> 00:05:33.999
-And is that a situation
where you guys

00:05:33.999 --> 00:05:36.535
had to reach out
and basically wait to see if

00:05:36.535 --> 00:05:40.272
Van Morrison
would sign off on it?

00:05:40.272 --> 00:05:42.775
-Well, yeah, but it wasn't
in the script.

00:05:42.775 --> 00:05:45.745
It all -- That all comes during
post-production.

00:05:45.745 --> 00:05:49.115
The show is put together,
and, you know,

00:05:49.115 --> 00:05:50.950
we had been thinking about
musical things.

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:53.919
And -- I forget.

00:05:53.919 --> 00:05:58.557
There was a -- there was a
contestant for that,

00:05:58.557 --> 00:06:02.395
which was a Lou Reed song.

00:06:02.395 --> 00:06:05.330
I don't remember the name,
but it was very close.

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:10.002
-You had another contestant
for "Don't Stop Believin'",

00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:13.539
which obviously now will
forever be tied to the show,

00:06:13.539 --> 00:06:15.007
because that's how it ended.

00:06:15.007 --> 00:06:16.108
And "Don't Stop Believin'"

00:06:16.108 --> 00:06:19.078
was not the crew's choice,
is this correct?

00:06:19.078 --> 00:06:20.445
-No, the crew didn't like it.

00:06:20.445 --> 00:06:25.317
I mean, we were
prepping the show

00:06:25.317 --> 00:06:27.753
and we were riding around
in a location van.

00:06:27.753 --> 00:06:29.956
It's about 12 people.

00:06:29.956 --> 00:06:33.226
And I never did this
with the crew before.

00:06:33.226 --> 00:06:35.394
But just to break
the tedium up, I said,

00:06:35.394 --> 00:06:37.330
"Alan and I have been thinking
about the last song."

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:38.831
And I was serious.

00:06:38.831 --> 00:06:42.634
"And I've got about
three contestants.

00:06:42.634 --> 00:06:44.036
What do you guys think?"

00:06:44.036 --> 00:06:45.404
"Oh, what are they?"

00:06:45.404 --> 00:06:47.339
So I told them what
the first one was,

00:06:47.339 --> 00:06:50.709
which was Al Green's song
"Love and Happiness."

00:06:50.709 --> 00:06:53.646
"Well, I don't see
how that works in."

00:06:53.646 --> 00:06:56.114
And then I said -- I forget
what the second one was.

00:06:56.114 --> 00:06:58.183
And then I said
"Don't Stop Believin'".

00:06:58.183 --> 00:07:01.587
They went, "Oh, Jesus, God, no.
How can you do that?

00:07:01.587 --> 00:07:03.790
Don't do that. That's terrible.
That's horrible."

00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:05.757
So, I said,
"Well, that's the one."

00:07:05.757 --> 00:07:09.394
And I didn't -- I didn't do that
to be mean or --

00:07:09.394 --> 00:07:13.298
I just thought,
"It is my favorite, actually,

00:07:13.298 --> 00:07:15.267
and I think there's
something there."

00:07:15.267 --> 00:07:18.337
It got such an emotional thing,
so, yeah, it was.

00:07:18.337 --> 00:07:19.772
-Well, I think if
it's your favorite --

00:07:19.772 --> 00:07:24.443
-Oh, and Steven Van Zandt was,
like -- Just wanted to shoot me.

00:07:24.443 --> 00:07:27.813
-Really? Stevie was against it?

00:07:27.813 --> 00:07:29.648
-Oh, totally.

00:07:29.648 --> 00:07:32.551
Totally against it.
-We've been talking about him.

00:07:32.551 --> 00:07:35.987
I feel like when that --
When the show started --

00:07:35.987 --> 00:07:38.290
And, again, I don't --

00:07:38.290 --> 00:07:40.359
You know, I don't remember
how I went into it.

00:07:40.359 --> 00:07:43.328
I think that maybe
the premise of the show,

00:07:43.328 --> 00:07:46.598
as it was pitched at the time
when it premiered on HBO,

00:07:46.598 --> 00:07:49.168
was a little comic --
the idea of, like,

00:07:49.168 --> 00:07:51.070
a mobster going to see
a therapist.

00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:54.607
And Little Stevie, to me, seemed
like comic casting,

00:07:54.607 --> 00:07:55.741
and then, as the show went on,

00:07:55.741 --> 00:07:57.910
I forgot it was
Steven Van Zandt.

00:07:57.910 --> 00:08:00.446
Like, he just became
Silvio to me.

00:08:00.446 --> 00:08:03.482
Did he surpass your expectations

00:08:03.482 --> 00:08:05.851
for what he would do
as an actor in that role?

00:08:05.851 --> 00:08:08.687
-Well, it was great watching him
develop as an actor.

00:08:08.687 --> 00:08:12.191
He was good
right from the first.

00:08:12.191 --> 00:08:14.293
You've probably heard this,
that I thought --

00:08:14.293 --> 00:08:16.395
I read him, we auditioned him,

00:08:16.395 --> 00:08:18.664
and I thought maybe
he could be Tony.

00:08:18.664 --> 00:08:21.566
And if he was Tony,
it would be a funny --

00:08:21.566 --> 00:08:22.768
a different kind of show,

00:08:22.768 --> 00:08:27.339
like a live-action "Simpsons"
or something.

00:08:27.339 --> 00:08:30.209
And we took three guys there --

00:08:30.209 --> 00:08:36.448
him and Jim and Michael Rispoli.

00:08:36.448 --> 00:08:39.184
You know, and then once you see
Jim, it's like, you know,

00:08:39.184 --> 00:08:42.755
"Alright, we're spending money
on this show, okay?

00:08:42.755 --> 00:08:45.457
We are spending millions of
dollars on this show.

00:08:45.457 --> 00:08:48.628
It's got to be somebody
who's acted in a series before."

00:08:48.628 --> 00:08:50.930
So, yeah, it was pretty evident.

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:52.732
-I don't want to
steal your thunder,

00:08:52.732 --> 00:08:54.200
because, again,
I love the prequel,

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:58.003
but I did write a prequel to
"The Sopranos" before you.

00:08:58.003 --> 00:08:59.404
-Oh.

00:08:59.404 --> 00:09:01.607
-I wrote an "SNL" sketch called
"The Sopranos Diaries."

00:09:01.607 --> 00:09:05.377
-I remember that.
-There's Bobby Moynihan as Tony.

00:09:05.377 --> 00:09:08.380
That's Bill Hader,
Fred Armisen, Kate McKinnon.

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:09.982
Did you see this sketch?

00:09:09.982 --> 00:09:12.317
-I did. I did.
That was very funny.

00:09:12.317 --> 00:09:13.652
-Alright.
-Yeah.

00:09:13.652 --> 00:09:14.787
-I'm going to leave it there.
I don't really need --

00:09:14.787 --> 00:09:16.255
-You wrote the whole thing?

00:09:16.255 --> 00:09:17.790
-I wrote the whole thing
all by myself.

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:21.060
I mean, I feel like you did
a lot of the legwork.

00:09:23.261 --> 00:09:25.197
I feel like, in a vacuum,
it wouldn't have worked

00:09:25.197 --> 00:09:28.533
without "The Sopranos,"
so I guess co-wrote.

00:09:28.533 --> 00:09:29.668
We co-wrote it.

00:09:29.668 --> 00:09:32.838
-Alright. It was good.

00:09:32.838 --> 00:09:33.939
-You know, the last thing --

00:09:33.939 --> 00:09:36.008
You know, we had
Michael Gandolfini here.

00:09:36.008 --> 00:09:38.978
You know, one of the,
really, most magnetic parts

00:09:38.978 --> 00:09:42.381
about watching this prequel
is watching him play the part

00:09:42.381 --> 00:09:44.283
that his father made so famous

00:09:44.283 --> 00:09:47.486
and just on the level of
mannerisms that are the same.

00:09:47.486 --> 00:09:49.287
But, you know, one of the things
he told me is that

00:09:49.287 --> 00:09:53.759
you had to tell him to be
a little bit less angry

00:09:53.759 --> 00:09:54.960
in the scenes.

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:58.064
Was that an effort
to make sure you left room

00:09:58.064 --> 00:10:00.199
for the character to develop

00:10:00.199 --> 00:10:03.569
to who we saw
in the television show?

00:10:03.569 --> 00:10:06.538
-You know, I really don't
remember that conversation,

00:10:06.538 --> 00:10:12.244
but it might have been he wasn't
so good at being angry.

00:10:12.244 --> 00:10:14.213
It could have been that.

00:10:14.213 --> 00:10:19.452
But there had to be some
headroom, somewhere to go

00:10:19.452 --> 00:10:20.753
toward the end of the movie.

00:10:20.753 --> 00:10:22.387
I think that's what it was.

00:10:22.387 --> 00:10:25.591
-Well, he's fantastic.
The film is fantastic.

00:10:25.591 --> 00:10:27.493
And thanks so much
for making time for us, David.

00:10:27.493 --> 00:10:28.560
I really appreciate
talking to you.

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:31.397
-Thank you. Thank you.

00:10:31.397 --> 00:10:33.766
-"The Many Saints of Newark"
is in theaters and on HBO Max.

